      q: "A 32-year-old woman comes to the emergency department due to sudden-onset, left-sided facial droop and arm weakness. The patient has also had right leg swelling after a recent road trip. She has no chronic medical conditions, and her only medication is an oral contraceptive pill. CT scan of the head reveals an acute infarction in the right middle cerebral artery territory. Lower extremity ultrasonography demonstrates a right femoral vein thrombus. No obvious cardiac structural abnormalities are detected on echocardiography. Agitated normal saline is injected into a peripheral vein to evaluate for abnormal right-to-left shunting. No bubbles are seen in the left side of the heart after several minutes. Which of the following is most appropriate for increasing right-sided heart pressure to reveal an obscured intracardiac shunt?",
