      explanation:
        "This patient's light-headedness, muscle cramps, and increased bathroom use are likely due to diarrhea caused by laxative misuse. Laxatives increase secretion of potassium and water into the colon and are often used as a purging mechanism by patients with eating disorders (eg, bulimia nervosa) to prevent weight gain.\n\nUnlike most other causes of diarrhea, which characteristically present with metabolic acidosis from loss of bicarbonate in the stool, laxative misuse can also present with metabolic alkalosis:\n\n• Chronic laxative misuse often produces more profound hypokalemia than other causes of diarrhea. The depleted potassium stimulates intracellular acidosis (transcellular shift: K+ out of cells, H+ into cells), resulting in increased ammonia and H+ secretion by proximal and distal tubule cells, respectively. This leads to increased bicarbonate generation and resorption by the kidney.\n\n• Hypovolemia and severe hypokalemia both increase activation of the renin-angiotensin- aldosterone system, with high levels of aldosterone stimulating further H+ loss and bicarbonate generation by alpha-intercalated cells.\n\nThese 2 factors frequently overcome the loss of bicarbonate in the stool in patients with laxative- induced diarrhea, facilitating a net increase in serum bicarbonate.\n\n(Choices A, C, D and E) Diarrhea due to laxative misuse can also sometimes present with metabolic acidosis or normal acid-base status. However, neither increased serum potassium nor decreased aldosterone levels would occur due to the loss of potassium and water in the stool.\n\nEducational objective:\n\nLaxative misuse increases secretion of potassium and water into the colon and can lead to profound hypokalemia and hypovolemia (with a compensatory increase in serum aldosterone). Metabolic alkalosis is a potential consequence, which contrasts with most other types of diarrhea that predominantly cause metabolic acidosis.",
