      explanation:
        "Unlike women, who typically experience a precipitous drop in sex hormone levels at menopause, men generally experience slower, more variable declines with aging. Typical findings seen in normal aging include the following:\n\n• Slow decline in gonadal testosterone production with lower total and free testosterone levels\n\n• A compensatory rise in circulating LH levels\n\n• Increased hepatic synthesis (and rising serum levels) of sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG)\n\nCompared to women of similar age, men often report higher levels of sexual functioning, activity, and satisfaction as they age. However, men commonly experience mild, nonspecific changes in sexual function, including the following:\n\n• Decreased ejaculate volume\n\n• Increased erectile latency and sexual refractory period (ie, time from orgasm until another orgasm is possible)\n\n• Moderately impaired erectile function with increased stimulation needed to achieve an erection\n\nAt very advanced age (eg, >80), very low testosterone levels can lead to specific hypogonadal symptoms (eg, loss of libido, erectile failure). However, this patient has nonspecific symptoms that are common in aging and do not suggest underlying gonadal failure.\n\n(Choice B) Certain synthetic androgen analogues (eg, danazol) and progestins (eg, levonorgestrel) can displace testosterone from SHBG, increasing free testosterone without a significant change in SHBG levels. However, this pattern is not seen in normal aging.\n\n(Choice C) SHBG levels are suppressed in patients with insulin resistance, diabetes, and obesity. If the pituitary-gonadal axis is intact, total testosterone levels are reduced, but free testosterone levels are relatively preserved. However, this patient has a normal weight, and SHBG is likely increased at age 66.\n\n(Choice D) Testosterone suppresses SHBG synthesis. Excessive, unregulated testosterone (eg, exogenous androgen abuse) would usually be associated with low SHBG levels, but these values would not be seen in normal aging.\n\n(Choice E) Hyperthyroidism often causes increased production of SHBG independent of age; total testosterone increases to saturate the increased steroid hormone-binding sites, and free testosterone is unchanged. Although this patient likely has increased SHBG levels, his total and free testosterone levels are more likely decreased due to the effects of aging.\n\nEducational objective:\n\nNormal aging in men is associated with a slow decline in gonadal testosterone production, a compensatory rise in LH, and rising serum levels of sex hormone-binding globulin. Common, nonspecific symptoms include decreased ejaculate volume, increased erectile latency and sexual refractory time, and moderately impaired erectile function.",
