      explanation:
        "Bone density is determined by peak bone mass in early adulthood and the subsequent rate of bone loss with age. Osteoporosis is defined as bone density ≥2.5 standard deviations below the mean young adult peak (ie, T-score ≤ -2.5).\n\nThe subjects in Group A show a higher peak bone mass, lower rate of postpeak bone loss, and higher late-life bone mass than the subjects in Group B. These findings are consistent with the effects of higher lean body mass, which is protective against osteoporosis. Higher lean body mass is associated with higher peak and greater preservation of bone density. In addition, regular weight-bearing exercise is associated with higher bone density, likely due to favorable effects on bone remodeling due to mechanical loading of bones.\n\nIn contrast, factors associated with lower peak bone mass include genetic factors, chronic inflammatory disease, exposure to glucocorticoid medications, and sedentary lifestyle.\n\n(Choice A) Estrogen maintains bone density by increasing osteoblastic activity and decreasing osteoclastic activity. Loss of estrogen effect due to premature menopause or functional hypothalamic amenorrhea (eg, excessive exercise, prolonged calorie deficits) can cause accelerated bone loss.\n\n(Choice B) High alcohol intake is associated with lower bone mass.\n\n(Choice D) Patients with inflammatory bowel disease are at risk for osteoporosis due to recurrent glucocorticoid use; patients with Crohn disease who undergo extensive bowel resection also can develop malabsorption of vitamin D.\n\n(Choice E) Vitamin D is largely generated by conversion from 7-dehydrocholesterol by sunlight exposure in the skin. Individuals living at extreme northern or southern latitudes are at risk for vitamin D deficiency and lower bone density.\n\n(Choice F) A mutation in type I collagen leads to osteogenesis imperfecta, which is associated with increased fracture risk and normal to lower bone density.\n\nEducational objective:\n\nHigh lean body mass is associated with higher peak bone density and slower loss of bone density. Regular weight-bearing exercise also is associated with higher bone density, likely due to favorable effects on bone remodeling.",
